    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Four things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The five-digit code on the last keypad.</b> This one failed the second-read test outright. The whole-frame pass gave <b>43758</b>, a 5x crop of the same second gave <b>17750</b>, and a contact-sheet pass gave <b>43578</b>. At 720p the digit cells are only a few pixels wide and the display blooms, so this page prints no five-digit code at all. The <b>1200</b> on the earlier keypad is a different matter: that one read the same way twice, and it is printed.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. The date on the horoscope.</b> Two passes over the same page disagreed — one read it as May 10 to May 16, the other as May 20 to May 26 — so the masthead range is not quoted here. The zodiac grid and the glyphs on the wall are solid; the date is not, and a wrong date would send you looking for a clue that does not exist.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. The zodiac order on the suitcase lock.</b> The glyphs above the suitcase were counted as four by one pass and five by another, and the names read differently each time (one pass had Taurus, Gemini, Libra and Scorpio; another had Taurus, Gemini, Pisces, Virgo and Sagittarius). The mechanism is certain — match the painted glyphs to the horoscope page — but this page will not print a sequence it read two different ways.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. The ending and the reward.</b> The file runs 8:16. The last frames are the room with the door ajar and the exit icon showing, and there is no results card, no <i>level completed</i> banner and no coin figure anywhere in it. Any reward number quoted for the Basement did not come from this recording.
    </div>
